[size=12pt][b]Where Core is ahead, and why[/b][/size]

[table]
[tr][td][b]module[/b][/td][td][b]Core[/b][/td][td][b]bmc[/b][/td][td][b]ratio[/b][/td][td][b]why[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]GCS filter construct, 100,000 elements[/td][td]4.9 ms[/td][td]21.6 ms[/td][td][b]4.4x slower[/b][/td][td]our side also parses the 4 MB block and dedups its scripts; Core hashes ready-made elements[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]GCS filter header hash[/td][td]62 us[/td][td]119 us[/td][td][b]1.9x slower[/b][/td][td]SHA-256d over the encoded filter[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]BIP324 ECDH[/td][td]21.6 us[/td][td]39.7 us[/td][td][b]1.8x slower[/b][/td][td]a constant-time w=4 window vs libsecp256k1's GLV-split ecmult_const; was 3.2x[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]CheckBlock, block 413,567[/td][td]370 us[/td][td]669 us[/td][td][b]1.8x slower[/b][/td][td]Core does more per block (see tests/bench_checkblock.c)[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]BIP324 packet AEAD, 64 B[/td][td]2.29 ns/B[/td][td]3.81 ns/B[/td][td][b]1.7x slower[/b][/td][td]three single ChaCha20 blocks per small packet: their latency is the row[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]taproot script-path verify[/td][td]36.0 us[/td][td]50.1 us[/td][td][b]1.4x slower[/b][/td][td]ours spends a 2-leaf tree; Core a 1-leaf[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]ChaCha20, 64 B / Poly1305, 64 B[/td][td]0.79 / 0.44 ns/B[/td][td]0.94 / 0.53 ns/B[/td][td][b]1.2x slower[/b][/td][td]one block is a dependent chain either way[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]SHA-256, 1 MB / SHA-256d 64 B x1024 / merkle root[/td][td]0.36 ns/B / 45.8 us / 45.7 ns/leaf[/td][td]0.42 / 52.9 / 53.0[/td][td][b]1.15x slower[/b][/td][td]Core's 2-way SHA-NI interleave vs our 1-way[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]P2WPKH / taproot key-path verify[/td][td]20.4 / 20.8 us[/td][td]22.9 / 22.3 us[/td][td][b]1.1x slower[/b][/td][td]the sighash is recomputed per input; Core precomputes midstates per transaction[/td][/tr]
[/table]

[size=12pt][b]Verdict[/b][/size]

The consensus kernel -- signature verification, the hashes on the hot path, compact-block reconstruction, the block archive -- is at or ahead of Core. Since 09-27 the transport cipher, the MuHash set hash and the address encoder moved from behind to well ahead, and SHA-1 and SHA-512 from behind to ahead and parity. What is left behind is named: the BIP324 ECDH's window against a GLV split, single-block latency on 64-byte packets, a filter-construction row whose two sides do different work, and Core's 2-way SHA-NI interleave on long SHA-256 inputs. Every number, harness and script is in the repository (docs/devlog/BENCHMARKS.md, addendum 2026-09-28).
